# The procedural layer, verbatim from the source capability.
INSTRUCTIONS = '# blindspot — hunt the unknown-unknowns\n\nThe most valuable thing this skill produces is the gap the user could not have\nasked about. Generic checklists fail twice: they list things he already\nhas (wasting his trust) and miss the specific silent killer in HIS system.\nEverything below exists to prevent those two failures.\n\n## The method\n\n### 1. Ground in what actually exists — before any opinion\n\nRead the real system first: code, configs, docs, runbooks, git history, live\nstate. Build a one-paragraph inventory of what\'s already covered. A gap claim\nthat turns out to already exist destroys the report\'s credibility; a\nrecommendation list that never says "you already have X covered" reads as\nunread boilerplate. Budget real effort here — in a repo, that means opening\nfiles, not skimming the README. If ultracode/Workflow is available and the\nsystem is large, fan out readers per subsystem and adversarially verify\ncandidate gaps before reporting (a "gap" a verifier can refute with a file\nreference was never a gap).\n\n### 2. Walk the expert lenses\n\nThe core mechanic: examine the system as a series of domain veterans would,\neach asking one question — *"what does every mature version of this thing\nhave that this one doesn\'t?"* Pick the 5–8 lenses that fit the domain (add\nyour own; these are seeds, not a form to fill):\n\n| Lens | The veteran\'s reflex question |\n|---|---|\n| Release engineering | What happens when a bad version ships — can it self-heal without users acting? |\n| Silent killers | What breaks every user at once with NO release event (unpinned deps, expiring certs/tokens, upstream API sunsets)? |\n| Data durability | What user data exists, what versions its schema, what migrates it, what backs it up, what restores it? |\n| Security / supply chain | What runs code or installs packages on trust? What\'s signed, what\'s merely fetched? |\n| Ecosystem & compatibility | What contract do third parties (plugin/agent/API authors) rely on, and what enforces it across versions? |\n| Incident response | When it breaks at 2am, where do users look, and what\'s the rehearsed rollback? |\n| Bus factor | What knowledge or access exists only in one person\'s head or machine? |\n| Observability | When a user says "it\'s broken", what evidence can they hand over — and what does it leak? |\n| Growth / adoption | What makes the second user\'s experience worse than the first\'s (scaling cliffs, support load, docs drift)? |\n| One-way doors | Which of today\'s casual choices (names, formats, schemas, URLs) become expensive-forever contracts? |\n\nFor each lens, name the SPECIFIC missing thing in THIS system with evidence\n(file, behavior, absence), never the abstract practice. "You have no staged\nrollout" is noise; "every user\'s launcher auto-pulls main, so a bad release\nhits the whole fleet within hours and only a manual git revert saves them —\nthere is no remote latest-good manifest" is a finding.\n\n### 3. Rank by consequence, not interestingness\n\nOrder findings by (user impact × irreversibility × probability), and label\neach with its class:\n\n- **Silent killer** — breaks users with no triggering event, nothing to roll back\n- **One-way door** — cheap to fix today, a permanent regret in a year\n- **Single point of failure** — one machine, one token, one person\n- **Erosion** — degrades trust gradually (docs drift, flaky installs, stale mirrors)\n\nDrop anything that is merely "best practice" without a concrete failure story\nin this system. If it can\'t hurt the user\'s users, it doesn\'t make the list.\n\n### 4. Deliver the contract\n\nLead with the shortlist — the reader sees one screen first. Structure:\n\n1. **Build-now shortlist** (≤5): each one sentence — the gap, the concrete\n   failure it prevents, rough cost now vs. cost later. Flag any item that is\n   a new feature if a no-new-features rule is in force (check memory — such\n   items may need to route through an experiment lane instead of mainline).\n2. **Ranked findings** with class labels and evidence.\n3. **Already covered** — 3–6 things checked and found solid, with what\n   covers them. This section is what makes the rest believable.\n4. **One-way doors closing soon** — decisions to make deliberately this\n   month even if the answer is "keep as is".\n\n## Failure modes to avoid\n\n- Proposing what already exists (you didn\'t read enough — go back to step 1).\n- The consultant dump: twenty framework-shaped bullets with no file paths.\n- Overfitting to the last domain you analyzed — re-derive the lens set fresh\n  for each system (a release train, a sales deck, and a family estate plan\n  share almost no lenses).\n- Ranking by intellectual interest instead of user pain.\n- Hedging the shortlist. The user is explicitly delegating judgment — commit to\n  an ordering and say why #1 is #1.'

